extreme drought conditions persist in  northern mexico  the city of monterrey home to some 5  million people has been particularly  hard hit  people  people are fighting over the water the  trucks arrive and people don't respect  the line it's chaos in our neighborhood  it's sad but what can we do we need a  miracle  these are the driest conditions the  region has seen in 30 years with  recorded rainfall levels far below  average  during a recent visit to monterrey  mexico's president andres manuel lopez  obrador promised to continue to provide  emergency government assistance  i believe this situation will help lead  everyone towards participating in the  conservation of water so that we will be  more conscious about the importance of  this vital resource  experts say as much as 65 percent of  mexico's territory is currently  experiencing moderate to severe drought  conditions  they attribute it in part to this year's  unseasonable la nina weather pattern  which has pushed rain and cooler  temperatures further north  extreme heat and a lack of rain are also  wreaking havoc across the border in the  united states prompting action from  officials there the growing drought  crisis is fueled by the impacts of  climate change including extreme heat  low precipitation  in turn severe drought conditions that  perpetuate the climate crisis by  increasing wildfire risk affecting crop  yields and ultimately disrupting our  ecosystems and the climate as a whole  u.s states like arizona and nevada have  already been asked to cut back what they  take from the drought-stricken colorado  river which supplies water to some 40  million people across seven u.s states  and to communities in northwestern  mexico  less water from the colorado river is  also raising longer term concerns in  both the u.s and mexico over potential  food shortages if action is not taken to  address the crisis manuel rapalo al  jazeera mexico city
 

